1.4
The pricing kernel
We saw in Section (1.3.3) that risk neutral returns were risk-adjusted natural returns. That is, the return under the
risk-neutral measure, Q, is the return under the natural measure, P, with the risk premium subtracted out. Hence, to
use risk neutral prices to estimate natural probabilities, we must know the risk adjustment to add it back in. This is
equivalent to knowing both the agent’s risk aversion and his subjective probability, which are non-observable. Various
authors tried to infer these risks from the market with the help of a model, but with more or less success. Further, the
40
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=2543802

Quantitative Analytics
natural expected return of a strategy depends on the risk premium for that strategy, so that knowledge on the kernel
can help in estimating the variability of the risk premium. At last, we are unable to obtain the current market forecast
of the expected returns on equities directly from their prices, and we are left to using historical returns. Even though
the risk premium is not directly observable from option prices various authors tried to infer it. This is because there is
a rich market in equity option prices and a well developed theory to extract the martingale or risk neutral probabilities
from these prices (see Ait-Sahalia et al. [1998]). As a result, one can use these probabilities to forecast the probability
distribution of future returns.
1.4.1
Deﬁning the pricing kernel
The asset pricing kernel summarises investor preferences for payoffs over different states of the world. In absence of
arbitrage, all asset prices can be expressed as the expected value of the product of the pricing kernel and the asset payoff
(see Equation (1.2.3)). Discounting payoffs using time and risk preferences, it is also called the stochastic discount
factor. Hence, combined with a probability model for the states, the pricing kernel gives a complete description of
asset prices, expected returns, and risk premia.
First we assume a complete market with a continuum of option prices in the state space. Second, we assume the
existence of a risk-neutral measure under which the expected return on any asset is the risk-free rate, and the existence
of a strictly positive pricing kernel. Then, we assume that the underlying process is Markov in the state variables, and
that we can discretise the state space. In a discrete time world with asset payoffs hpXq at time T, contingent on the
realisation of a state of nature X P Ω, absence of arbitrage opportunity (AAO) (see Dybvig et al. [2003]) implies the
existence of positive state space prices, that is, the Arrow-Debreu contingent claims prices, ppXq, paying $1 in state X
and nothing in any other states (see Theorem (E.1.1)). In general spaces, we get the price distribution function PpXq
where dPpXq  ppXqdX. If the market is complete, then these state prices are unique.
Following Ross [2013], we let the current value πh of an asset paying hpXq in one period be given by
