The population kurtosis
The kurtosis is a measure of the peakedness of a probability distribution of random variables. As such, it discloses how concentrated a return distribution is around the mean. Higher kurtosis means more of
the variance is due to infrequent extreme deviations (fat tails), lower kurtosis implies a variance composed of frequent
modestly-sized deviations. Normally distributed asset returns exhibit a kurtosis of 3. To measure the excess kurtosis
with regard to a normal distribution, a value of 3 is hence subtracted from the kurtosis value. A distribution with
positive excess kurtosis is called leptokurtic.
